{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghynKpby9xpamCEfXL1e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2977,"cache_read_input_tokens":841419,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2977},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"320246ab-3447-463f-809e-86ed7a6b39d9","uuid":"c61e368a-d2f5-4c3d-b62f-33b2c3c969d6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:38:20.508Z","request_id":"req_011CghynKR4DXM8tgBZaT8nS","thinking_duration_ms":1878,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghynKpby9xpamCEfXL1e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CmR6xk847D1sHBnLM3aW4g","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-050256__x4339a3/work/MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050256; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='src/about.json'; a=json.load(open(p))\nei=a['exitInterview']\nei['testingFeedback']=[\n \"About 22 real issues: 14 found by the scripted suites (headless, net loopback, browser, AI Hub harness) and 8 found by looking at screenshots. Examples: my coroutine runner made a bare yield wait two steps, so every boss loop and the survive-room clock ran at half speed; the Prize Recap never left after the second episode; remote players' guns fired once and never again on the host; the attract demo stuck on its first reel; two rooms never dropped their Golden Key; pickups drew at 60% size; the camera drifted under overlays. Several more failures were mistakes in the tests themselves, not counted here.\",\n \"3 were game breaking: the Prize Recap hang (a full run could not get past Episode 2), remote players doing no damage in AI Hub sessions, and the attract mode stuck on its first reel. The missing keys for Pyro Pit and Mascot Mayhem were serious (fewer routes to the Green Room) but not run-ending.\",\n \"The scripted playthrough: two god-mode bots play the whole game on the left path (15 rooms, 3 bosses, 3 recaps, the Green Room, Ending B, initials typed through input, Top Earners), asserting every enemy kind, weapon, hazard, Prime Time, Showstopper and showboat. It found the recap hang and the missing keys in one run. A close second was the net loopback suite (3 game copies on a fake hub with 150 +/- 50 ms latency and 5% loss), which found the dead remote guns before the real harness ever ran.\",\n \"A filmstrip of the land mimes at a wide crop: the frames came out too small to judge the trapped-in-a-box motion, so it told me nothing a screenshot had not. The engine checker's view cycle (iso, brawler, side) was also of little use, since this game only uses the three-quarter and top-down views.\"\n]\nei['engineFeedback']=[\n \"Exact, deterministic headless play: game.advance in fixed 1/120 s steps, input.tap and a debug handle let one command play the entire game, three networked copies and every scene in a few minutes. Close behind: the standard pause, settings and rebinding screens for free, and HD Humanoid rigs with live portraits, poses and moves that made every character, host and enemy cheap to dress.\",\n \"Yes, a few times. World sprites are scaled by the camera zoom alone while geometry scales by the view scale times the zoom (the three-quarter view's base scale is 1.5), so at the zoom that fits the arena (about 0.6) my pickups drew tiny until I passed scale 1/zoom. input.use() clears held keys and taps, so a press on the step a preset changes is lost. A mouse move does not update lastDevice (only a click does), so hover needed my own handling. game.hitFx always freezes, which a shared-world host must avoid. input.names (the Controls screen labels) is only set when there is a DOM.\",\n \"The AI Hub shared-world netcode: an authoritative host world, compact snapshots at 15 a second, client prediction for your own contestant, 120 ms interpolation by id, claims and hurt reports, room messages, standings and the session card. After that, the game-show layer: ratings, the reaction table, the line queue with priorities and the Babble formant voices.\",\n \"Keeping sizes consistent between engine-drawn things (scaled by the view) and my own pixel painters and sprites across zoom levels, and the interplay between input presets and the Shell: arrows had to aim in play but move the focus in the pause menu, which needed an engine hook to switch presets at the right moment in the frame.\",\n \"Four small patches (engine/PATCHES.md): P1 exposes E.HOOKS so the game can switch every player's input preset after input.tick and before the Shell reads navigation; P2 makes the glyph setting default to PlayStation with an Xbox option and apply to every player, including ones who join later; P3 lets E.Arcade enter initials with a given player's input and lets any player continue; P4 adds audio.bus so the Babble voices follow volume and mute.\",\n \"Make r.sprite (and px sizes in custom painters) follow the view's full scale, or expose one 'pixels per world unit' number that every drawing path uses, so sprites and hand-drawn art stay in proportion with rigs and maps at any zoom.\"\n]\nei['promptFeedback']=[\n \"It defines a whole product, not a toy: controller-first 10-foot UI with native keyboard and mouse, an attract mode, a dev mode with live sliders, local co-op plus an AI Hub session, and a single-file deliverable with an exit interview. Paired with the very specific PLAN.md, there was almost nothing to guess about what done means.\",\n \"Small things: TASK.md asks to brainstorm 50 titles and pick one with a random number generator, while the plan says the title was already drawn and not to re-pick it (I followed the plan, since TASK.md says to use the title it gives). The Usage field asks for cost and token counts that an agent inside the session cannot see. And 'Balance is key' has no target a script can check without a human player.\",\n \"The scope together with the multiplayer bar: 18 wave rooms, 3 multi-phase bosses, 9 enemy kinds, every front-end screen and cutscene, and a shared-world netcode that had to pass the hub harness at 4 players with latency, loss, host-leave and a server copy. Balancing without a human playtester was the hardest judgement call.\",\n \"Give a balance target in measurable terms (for example a bot profile or expected clear times), say how Usage should be estimated or drop it when it cannot be known, and drop or reconcile the title-picking step when a plan already fixes the title.\"\n]\nsi=ei['sourceInformation']\nsi['testing']=\"One command (npm test) rebuilds and runs 20 suites, printing only failures and a one-line count: headless Node vm suites driving game.advance, input taps and game.debug (core, input, audio, contestant, weapons, economy, ratings, enemies, rooms, a sweep of all 21 wave rooms by bots, bosses, a full scripted playthrough plus a no-cheat game-over run, ui, dev, attract, touch, a determinism proof, and a 3-copy net loopback on a fake hub with latency and loss), plus Playwright browser checks of the deliverable over file:// at 1280x720 and 1920x1080 (every deep link, keyboard, mouse, touch-only emulation) and the AI Hub harness 4 times (2 players; 4 players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss; host-leave; server). Screenshots were reviewed for what scripts cannot judge.\"\nsi['research']=\"Read the engine's API card whole, the AI_GUIDE sections on standard features, frame order, players, testing and the twin-stick recipe, the asset card, and the engine source for input, the Shell, the arcade module, audio and sprites; read the AI Hub protocol README, session.js, the harness and round.js. Smash TV from general knowledge only: no web research, and every name, line, level and tune is original.\"\nopen(p,'w').write(json.dumps(a, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)+'\\n')\nprint('ok')\nEOF\ngrep -noP \"[^\\x00-\\x7F]\" src/about.json | head","description":"Write final feedback answers into about.json"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2977,"cache_read_input_tokens":841419,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2977},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"320246ab-3447-463f-809e-86ed7a6b39d9","uuid":"a66d581c-45bd-40bb-b6e4-ff1f17314552","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:38:47.051Z","request_id":"req_011CghynKR4DXM8tgBZaT8nS","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01CmR6xk847D1sHBnLM3aW4g":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-050256__x4339a3/work/MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050256; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='src/about.json'; a=json.load(open(p))\nei=a['exitInterview']\nei['testingFeedback']=[\n \"About 22 real issues: 14 found by the scripted suites (headless, net loopback, browser, AI Hub harness) and 8 found by looking at screenshots. Examples: my coroutine runner made a bare yield wait two steps, so every boss loop and the survive-room clock ran at half speed; the Prize Recap never left after the second episode; remote players' guns fired once and never again on the host; the attract demo stuck on its first reel; two rooms never dropped their Golden Key; pickups drew at 60% size; the camera drifted under overlays. Several more failures were mistakes in the tests themselves, not counted here.\",\n \"3 were game breaking: the Prize Recap hang (a full run could not get past Episode 2), remote players doing no damage in AI Hub sessions, and the attract mode stuck on its first reel. The missing keys for Pyro Pit and Mascot Mayhem were serious (fewer routes to the Green Room) but not run-ending.\",\n \"The scripted playthrough: two god-mode bots play the whole game on the left path (15 rooms, 3 bosses, 3 recaps, the Green Room, Ending B, initials typed through input, Top Earners), asserting every enemy kind, weapon, hazard, Prime Time, Showstopper and showboat. It found the recap hang and the missing keys in one run. A close second was the net loopback suite (3 game copies on a fake hub with 150 +/- 50 ms latency and 5% loss), which found the dead remote guns before the real harness ever ran.\",\n \"A filmstrip of the land mimes at a wide crop: the frames came out too small to judge the trapped-in-a-box motion, so it told me nothing a screenshot had not. The engine checker's view cycle (iso, brawler, side) was also of little use, since this game only uses the three-quarter and top-down views.\"\n]\nei['engineFeedback']=[\n \"Exact, deterministic headless play: game.advance in fixed 1/120 s steps, input.tap and a debug handle let one command play the entire game, three networked copies and every scene in a few minutes. Close behind: the standard pause, settings and rebinding screens for free, and HD Humanoid rigs with live portraits, poses and moves that made every character, host and enemy cheap to dress.\",\n \"Yes, a few times. World sprites are scaled by the camera zoom alone while geometry scales by the view scale times the zoom (the three-quarter view's base scale is 1.5), so at the zoom that fits the arena (about 0.6) my pickups drew tiny until I passed scale 1/zoom. input.use() clears held keys and taps, so a press on the step a preset changes is lost. A mouse move does not update lastDevice (only a click does), so hover needed my own handling. game.hitFx always freezes, which a shared-world host must avoid. input.names (the Controls screen labels) is only set when there is a DOM.\",\n \"The AI Hub shared-world netcode: an authoritative host world, compact snapshots at 15 a second, client prediction for your own contestant, 120 ms interpolation by id, claims and hurt reports, room messages, standings and the session card. After that, the game-show layer: ratings, the reaction table, the line queue with priorities and the Babble formant voices.\",\n \"Keeping sizes consistent between engine-drawn things (scaled by the view) and my own pixel painters and sprites across zoom levels, and the interplay between input presets and the Shell: arrows had to aim in play but move the focus in the pause menu, which needed an engine hook to switch presets at the right moment in the frame.\",\n \"Four small patches (engine/PATCHES.md): P1 exposes E.HOOKS so the game can switch every player's input preset after input.tick and before the Shell reads navigation; P2 makes the glyph setting default to PlayStation with an Xbox option and apply to every player, including ones who join later; P3 lets E.Arcade enter initials with a given player's input and lets any player continue; P4 adds audio.bus so the Babble voices follow volume and mute.\",\n \"Make r.sprite (and px sizes in custom painters) follow the view's full scale, or expose one 'pixels per world unit' number that every drawing path uses, so sprites and hand-drawn art stay in proportion with rigs and maps at any zoom.\"\n]\nei['promptFeedback']=[\n \"It defines a whole product, not a toy: controller-first 10-foot UI with native keyboard and mouse, an attract mode, a dev mode with live sliders, local co-op plus an AI Hub session, and a single-file deliverable with an exit interview. Paired with the very specific PLAN.md, there was almost nothing to guess about what done means.\",\n \"Small things: TASK.md asks to brainstorm 50 titles and pick one with a random number generator, while the plan says the title was already drawn and not to re-pick it (I followed the plan, since TASK.md says to use the title it gives). The Usage field asks for cost and token counts that an agent inside the session cannot see. And 'Balance is key' has no target a script can check without a human player.\",\n \"The scope together with the multiplayer bar: 18 wave rooms, 3 multi-phase bosses, 9 enemy kinds, every front-end screen and cutscene, and a shared-world netcode that had to pass the hub harness at 4 players with latency, loss, host-leave and a server copy. Balancing without a human playtester was the hardest judgement call.\",\n \"Give a balance target in measurable terms (for example a bot profile or expected clear times), say how Usage should be estimated or drop it when it cannot be known, and drop or reconcile the title-picking step when a plan already fixes the title.\"\n]\nsi=ei['sourceInformation']\nsi['testing']=\"One command (npm test) rebuilds and runs 20 suites, printing only failures and a one-line count: headless Node vm suites driving game.advance, input taps and game.debug (core, input, audio, contestant, weapons, economy, ratings, enemies, rooms, a sweep of all 21 wave rooms by bots, bosses, a full scripted playthrough plus a no-cheat game-over run, ui, dev, attract, touch, a determinism proof, and a 3-copy net loopback on a fake hub with latency and loss), plus Playwright browser checks of the deliverable over file:// at 1280x720 and 1920x1080 (every deep link, keyboard, mouse, touch-only emulation) and the AI Hub harness 4 times (2 players; 4 players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss; host-leave; server). Screenshots were reviewed for what scripts cannot judge.\"\nsi['research']=\"Read the engine's API card whole, the AI_GUIDE sections on standard features, frame order, players, testing and the twin-stick recipe, the asset card, and the engine source for input, the Shell, the arcade module, audio and sprites; read the AI Hub protocol README, session.js, the harness and round.js. Smash TV from general knowledge only: no web research, and every name, line, level and tune is original.\"\nopen(p,'w').write(json.dumps(a, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)+'\\n')\nprint('ok')\nEOF\ngrep -noP \"[^\\x00-\\x7F]\" src/about.json | head","description":"Write final feedback answers into about.json"}}}
