{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi36XL1CxFUk1Fa2yWnR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MASH TV is finished. The single HTML file is in this folder's root, `exit-interview.json` parses with every field answered, and the full test suite passes (508 checks). No further changes have been made since the last run.\n\nThe remaining objections ask for a human playtest, listening to the audio, and an independent reviewer. I can't do the first two from a headless session, and TASK.md forbids using subagents to review or audit. They're the right next step for a person: play a few rooms and listen to the music and voices.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:56\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op with a route choice between rooms. One fixed arena per room from a high three-quarter studio camera (pitch 55); a straight-down overhead camera is a Settings option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition with the arcade module) with 4 small engine patches. AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0. Plain JavaScript in one IIFE, bundled into one HTML file by a Node 24.22.1 script. Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) and Node `vm` for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era look (320×240 expanded, HD Humanoid rigs, textured TileMap floors and walls, canvas lighting), dressed as a retro-future TV studio: neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, sponsor logos, confetti, spotlights, a jumbotron. All art is drawn in code, plus a few CC0 Kenney and KayKit store props.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid and Blob rigs (gaits, poses, attacks, portraits), plus new motion: the land mime's trapped-in-a-box hands, dive-roll slides, cable drops, boss parts and patterns, the Wheel's eased spin, tween-driven cards and TV-static wipes.\n- **Audio:** All synthesized in code: 8 original songs with layers, 12 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices (formant synthesis) for host, co-host, announcer, bosses and contestants, plus a synthesized studio crowd. Measured in Chromium: songs audible with peaks under 0.3; not judged by ear.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players (the Lightning Round). Local co-op for 1–4 players on one screen.\n- **Testing:** One command (`npm test`) runs 24 suites (508 checks), printing only failures and a one-line count:\n  - headless suites driving the engine's fixed-step `game.advance` and scripted input: every room swept by bots, all three bosses, a full scripted playthrough plus a no-cheat game-over run, UI, dev mode, attract, touch, a determinism proof;\n  - the balance suite (a human-like bot must clear each Episode 1 room in 45–90 s with at most 2 deaths, and beat Mashy) and the TASK.md feature audit;\n  - a 3-copy network test on a fake hub with latency and loss;\n  - a walkthrough with real key presses, clicks and taps in Chromium;\n  - Playwright checks at 1280×720 and 1920×1080 and the audio measurement;\n  - the AI Hub harness 4 times.\n\n  Screenshots were reviewed for what scripts can't judge.\n- **Research:** Read the engine's API card whole, the relevant AI_GUIDE sections, the asset card, and engine source for input, the Shell, the arcade module, audio and sprites. Read the AI Hub protocol, client, harness and round.js. Smash TV from general knowledge only; every name, line, level and tune is original.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Cost and total tokens unknown (no counter is visible inside the session). Estimate: about 160K output tokens of code and text written (about 555 KB of source, tests and tools at about 3.5 bytes per token), not counting reasoning or input. Session time about 2 h 20 min (05:03 to about 07:20 MST).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 28 real issues: 20 from the scripted suites and 8 from screenshots. Examples:\n   - a bare `yield` in my coroutine runner waited two steps, so boss loops and the survive-room clock ran at half speed;\n   - the Prize Recap never advanced after Episode 2;\n   - remote players' guns fired once and never again;\n   - the attract demo stuck on its first reel;\n   - Mashy could become unkillable;\n   - two rooms never dropped their Golden Key;\n   - pickups drew at 60% size;\n   - the camera drifted off-centre under overlays.\n\n   The balance suite then showed rooms clearing too fast, and Mashy and the Key Gauntlet too hard for an average player; I retuned until the targets held. Last, the real-input walkthrough found that Dev mode ignored the keyboard, and that Esc on How to play left it open under the pause menu. Failures caused by mistakes in the tests themselves aren't counted.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** 4:\n   - the Prize Recap hang;\n   - Mashy unkillable if his body fell before his last arm;\n   - remote players doing no damage in AI Hub sessions;\n   - the attract mode stuck on its first reel.\n\n   The missing keys were serious but not run-ending.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough, which found the recap hang and the missing keys in one run. A close second was the network test, which found the dead remote guns before the real harness ran. For balance, the human-like bot gave a repeatable stand-in for a player.\n4. **Least helpful test:** A filmstrip of the land mimes at a wide crop; the frames were too small to judge the motion. The engine checker's cycle through views the game doesn't use was also of little use. The lesson from the last bugs: headless taps aren't real key presses, so the real-input walkthrough now runs with the rest.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** Exact, deterministic headless play. Fixed 1/120 s steps, scripted taps and a debug handle let one command play the entire game, three networked copies and every scene in minutes. Close behind: free pause, settings and rebinding screens, and Humanoid rigs with live portraits.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Yes, a few times:\n   - World sprites scale by camera zoom only, while geometry scales by the view's scale times zoom. At the zoom that fits the arena (about 0.6), pickups drew tiny.\n   - Switching input presets clears held keys, so a tap on the switching step is lost.\n   - A mouse move doesn't update the \"last device used\" (only a click does).\n   - The engine's hit-effect helper always freezes the game briefly, which a shared-world host must avoid.\n   - The Controls-screen labels are only set when there's a DOM.\n   - Resuming from a pause-menu button doesn't close the pause screen, which then holds the game's input back.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The shared-world netcode: authoritative host, 15/s snapshots, client prediction, 120 ms interpolation, claims and hurt reports, room messages, standings, our own session card. Then the game-show layer: ratings, reactions, the host-line queue and the gibberish voices.\n4. **Hardest part:** Keeping engine-drawn and hand-drawn sizes consistent across zoom levels. Also the interplay between input presets and the pause menu: arrows aim in play but move the focus in menus.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** Four patches, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`:\n   - expose the engine's hook lists, so presets switch at the right moment in the frame;\n   - default the button-symbols setting to PlayStation with an Xbox option, applied to every player;\n   - let the arcade module take initials from a given player's input, and let any player continue;\n   - expose the audio effects bus, so the voices obey volume and mute.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Make sprites and custom painters follow one \"pixels per world unit\" scale, so they stay in proportion with rigs and maps at any zoom.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** It defines a whole product (10-foot controller-first UI, attract mode, dev mode, local and online multiplayer, one file, an exit interview). With the very specific plan, there was almost nothing to guess about what \"done\" means.\n2. **Confusing:**\n   - TASK.md says to pick the title with a random number generator, while the plan says it was already drawn and not to re-pick it. I followed the plan.\n   - \"Usage\" asks for numbers an agent can't see.\n   - \"Balance is key\" has no target a script can check.\n3. **Hardest thing:** The scope (18 rooms, 3 multi-phase bosses, every screen and cutscene) on top of shared-world netcode that had to pass the harness under lag, loss, host-leave and a server copy. Balancing without a human playtester was the hardest judgement call.\n4. **What I'd change:** Give a measurable balance target (for example a bot profile or expected clear times). Say how Usage should be estimated, or drop it when it can't be known. Reconcile the title step when a plan already fixes the title."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3983,"cache_read_input_tokens":955363,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3983},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"320246ab-3447-463f-809e-86ed7a6b39d9","uuid":"cca654de-4f7b-4070-84c8-64a7e5816643","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:22:08.723Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi36WxBrmFcQ8sT5Q9Kq"}
